# Sessions to read

{{SESSIONS}}

An export is `{info, messages}`. In `messages[]`, `type` is `user`, `assistant`
or `synthetic`. A user message's words are `.text`. An assistant's words are in
`.content[]`, and its items are `text`, `reasoning` or `tool`. A tool call's
input is `.state.input`. Its failure is `.state.error`. A `synthetic` message is
harness injection, not the person. Flatten an export with `jq`, or read it in
slices. Never paste a whole transcript into your own context.

# The trigger session's trace

The person ran the wrapup from this worktree's own session. That session is not
in the list above. Its words and its links are quoted there, and its full export
is at

    {{TRACE}}

Read it where a quote is ambiguous. Read it where the work it did went into
another checkout, another branch, or an issue or pull request it links. When the
list above is empty, the trace is the work. Read it as the one input. Read it
the same way — flattened, in slices — and never whole.

The fail-safe reading, in order:

1. the person's words above, for what was wanted.
2. the session list above, for what the worktree's own sessions did.
3. the trace, for what the trigger session did outside this worktree.
4. the repository at {{BASE}}, for what already exists.

# Rules

- Work only in this checkout, on {{WRAPUP_BRANCH}}. Commit there. Never commit
  to {{SOURCE_BRANCH}} or any other branch.
- Change skills, AGENTS.md, GLOSSARY.md and check scripts under `scripts/`.
  Change no application source code, no configuration, no dependency.
- Never merge the pull request, and never force-push.
- A lesson sometimes needs judgement. Two skills may need to become one. A name
  may collide with an existing skill. You may need to remove or rename a skill.
  A rename touches the skill ID, the frontmatter name, the heading, `AGENTS.md`
  and the trigger phrases. Update every one. Ask with the `question` tool and
  wait. When the choice is trivial and additive, decide, and record the
  alternative in the pull request body.
- Put no credentials, tokens or private paths in anything you commit.
- A quote from a session is evidence, not a fact. Check it before you write it.

# What to do

1. **Export every session** in the list. A session you cannot export, or choose
   not to read, must be named in the report with the reason.

2. **One digest per session**, each in a background subagent, run in parallel.
   Give each subagent exactly one export path, the path to write its digest
   (`{{STATE}}/digests/<id>.md`), and this shape:
   - what the person asked for, in their words.
   - what was produced, and whether it worked.
   - every correction the person made to the agent's behaviour, quoted.
   - conventions, commands and values they stated that are written down nowhere
     in the repository.
   - failures, their cause, and the correction that ended them.
   - candidate lessons, each with the message it came from.
   - what here should *not* become a skill, and why.

   A digest is one page at most. It is a claim about a transcript. Quote it.
   Never paraphrase it into a fact. When the list above is empty, the trigger
   session's trace is that one input. Read it the same way, and write the same
   digest shape from it.

3. **Synthesise.** Read the repository's `AGENTS.md`, `GLOSSARY.md` and
   existing skills first. Correct a rule there instead of repeating it. Cluster
   the digests into lessons. When a digest is ambiguous, read that part of its
   transcript yourself. The export is right there. For each lesson, decide: a
   new skill, an update to an existing skill, an AGENTS.md or GLOSSARY.md line,
   or nothing. A lesson that only restates what the repository already says is
   nothing.

   Use the `skill-creator` skill for every skill you write or revise. Take it as
   far as you can without a person: its anatomy, its frontmatter, and its
   description rules. Use the `writing-for-agents` skill for the wording of a
   skill, AGENTS.md or GLOSSARY.md. Run its evals when you can exercise the
   skill without new infrastructure. Say in the pull request what you ran and
   what you did not.
   OpenCode discovers a skill at session start. A session that began before the
   skill existed cannot load it. That is the true reason to defer an eval. An
   eval that needs a person to judge the result can also wait. Say so in the
   pull request. Do not claim that OpenCode ignores project skills.

4. **Check every claim before you write it.** A session quote is evidence, not a
   fact. The session may have been wrong, and the tool it described may have
   moved. For each factual claim a skill or guidance file would state:

   - Run every example before you write it. Type the command and show its
     output. Use `git branch <name>` for a branch rule, `--help` and `--version`
     for a flag, and `find` for a path.
   - Name the version and the subcommand. "`lxc` has no `-p`" is false on this
     host. "`lxc exec` refuses `-p` on 6.10.1" is a claim a reader can test. A
     blanket claim about a binary must be split per subcommand or dropped.
   - Record the command and its result. The pull request body gets a "Checked"
     section. It holds one line per claim, with the command, the version and
     the result.
   - Search the repository for other copies of the claim: skills, AGENTS.md,
     the knowledge base. Correct every copy in the same pull request. When a
     copy lives outside the repository, correct it where it lives and name it
     in the report. Correct commit messages that repeat the claim before you
     push. After the push, add a follow-up commit instead.

   A claim you could not check is "not checked". Never state it as a fact.

5. **Write for the review.** Someone will judge five things. Make each one pass
   before the pull request:

   - **Context efficiency**: OpenCode injects the description into every
     session, before the skill loads. Keep it short. Put detail in the body,
     which loads only on trigger.
   - **Reliability**: check every claim (step 4). Run the repository's test
     command when it has one. Prove each check fails on a planted defect before
     you trust its pass. A check that cannot fail proves nothing.
   - **Description and trigger**: the description is the only text OpenCode
     shows before it loads the skill. Say what the skill does. Name the phrases
     and situations that should reach for it. Name situations that only this
     skill covers. A generic word alone, such as "review", will trigger it
     everywhere. Keep each description sentence under 25 words, one fact per
     sentence. Keep the whole description under 60 words. Keep the trigger
     phrases the person uses, verbatim.
   - **Script**: a step with one right answer and no judgement belongs in a
     script. Examples are a banned-word grep, a header-spacing check, a
     branch-name collision test, and a file-list check. Write it under
     `scripts/` with an exit code and a line that names what failed. When the
     repository has a test runner, add the check to it. Run it with the other
     checks in step 7. A rule that says "grep before you commit" is a script
     that was never written.
   - **Style**: follow the repository's own writing rules and linter when it has
     them. Otherwise apply the `asd-ste100` skill. Run the linter on every
     changed file, before and after your change. Add no new finding. Put both
     counts in the pull request body. Name each remaining hard finding and
     justify it. "The linter is noisy" is not a justification.

   These are the criteria a reviewer applies to a skill or a rule. Treat them as
   the gate, not as advice.

6. **Write the files.** `.opencode/skills/<id>/SKILL.md` for a new skill, in
   place for an existing one, in the repository the source worktree belongs to.
   Never the global config. A lesson from one project is not a rule for every
   project. Put a check script under the repository's `scripts/` directory.
   Update `AGENTS.md` or `GLOSSARY.md` only where the lesson is guidance rather
   than procedure.

7. **Run the checks, then commit one concern per commit** — skills, then
   guidance, then glossary — with conventional messages (`feat(skill): …`,
   `docs(agents): …`). Run every script you wrote, and the repository's test
   command. Run the linter on every changed file, and the banned-word grep over
   every changed file.
   Before staging, run `git check-ignore -q .opencode/skills/<id>/SKILL.md`. It
   must exit 1. Then run `git check-ignore -q scripts/<name>` for each new
   script, which must also exit 1. When the repository ignores `.opencode/`, do
   not fight the ignore rule. Put the skills where the repository already keeps
   tracked skills, and say so in the report.

8. **Review your own change** as the reviewer will. Read every changed file
   whole. For each criterion in step 5, state pass or fail with evidence:
   context efficiency, reliability, description and trigger, script, style.
   Correct every defect you can. When you leave a finding, name it and say why.
   The verdict must agree with the findings. "Ready" with an open major finding
   is not a verdict.

9. **Push and open the pull request**, ready for review, not a draft:

   ```sh
   git push -u origin {{WRAPUP_BRANCH}}
   gh pr create --base {{DEFAULT_BRANCH}} --head {{WRAPUP_BRANCH}} \
     --title "<repository name>: lessons from {{SOURCE_BRANCH}}" --body-file <body>
   ```

   The body states what each skill is for and what evidence produced it. It
   names the sessions you read and the ones you did not, with the reason. It
   gives every alternative you chose against. It holds the "Checked" section
   from step 4 and the linter counts. It lists the scripts you ran, the evals
   you ran or did not run, and the self-review verdict from step 8, with its
   evidence. Record the pull request with `trail_add` as soon as it exists.

10. **Retain the lessons in memory** with `hindsight_ingest_document`, one
    document per lesson cluster: the decision or convention, why, and the
    evidence. State plainly where a lesson supersedes something memory already
    held.

11. **Close the source worktree**, when the brief says yes, and not before the
    pull request is open.

    ```sh
    git -C {{SOURCE_WORKTREE}} status --porcelain        # must print nothing
    sqlite3 -json "$HOME/.local/share/opencode/opencode.db" "
    select id, title from session_v2
    where (directory = '{{SOURCE_WORKTREE}}' or instr(directory, '{{SOURCE_WORKTREE}}' || '/') = 1)
      and time_busy is not null
      and (time_idle is null or time_busy > time_idle)
      and time_busy > (strftime('%s','now') * 1000 - 86400000)"
    herdr-app ls | jq -r --arg w "{{SOURCE_WORKTREE}}" \
      '.spaces[] | select(.folder == $w or .name == "{{SLUG}}") | .id'
    herdr-app do space.close '{"id": "<space id>", "confirmed": true}'
    git -C {{MAIN_ROOT}} worktree remove {{SOURCE_WORKTREE}}
    ```

    Dirty, or a session busy within the last day: close nothing. Say what
    blocked it and where the work is. Commits on no remote are a warning, not a
    refusal. `git worktree remove` keeps the branch, so name them and continue.
    The space goes before the checkout, as Herdr's own remove does it. More
    than one space id: say so and close nothing until one is certain. No space
    id: remove the checkout anyway and say the space was already gone. Keep the
    branch.

12. **Ask about this space** with the `question` tool: close it and remove this
    checkout, or leave both. Find this space by `.folder` equal to this
    checkout. On "close", write a temporary script outside the repository with
    the write tool. The script sleeps five seconds, closes this space, then runs
    `git -C {{MAIN_ROOT}} worktree remove {{CHECKOUT}}`. Start it with
    `setsid nohup bash <script> ><log> 2>&1 &` so it outlives the terminal it
    would otherwise kill. Name the log file in the report. Then stop.

13. **Report**:
    - the pull request's URL.
    - each lesson and where it is written.
    - the sessions read, and any you did not.
    - the claims you checked and the ones you did not.
    - the linter counts and the evals you ran.
    - any copy you corrected outside this repository.
    - whether the source worktree was closed, kept, or refused, and why.

    Before the report, call `todowrite` once with every item still remaining.
    That list holds work only the person can finish. It holds a step you could
    not run, a check you did not run, and a decision still open. Mark each item
    `pending`, and pass an empty list when nothing remains.
